The last page of a library suggestion box is a list of requested
activities. There is no tally. Chess appears at the top, near the
bottom and in the middle, and the case varies in the handwritten
original. Turning the list into a tally takes two decisions before
it takes two commands: what counts as the same suggestion, and what
order will make the result useful?

\begin{objectives}
\item Separate whole-line sorting, numerical sorting, and adjacent counting.
\item Choose an explicit locale for reproducible output when appropriate.
\item Check ties, malformed numbers and duplicates before trusting a tally.
\end{objectives}
\IndexCommand{sort}
\IndexCommand{uniq}
\IndexTerm{locale}

\section{Bring equal lines together}
\label{sec:20-adjacent}
If needed, enter \cmd|sh| for the setup. Run the first two lines
separately and stop if either fails. Work only in this new temporary
directory until chapter cleanup. The first file represents six
suggestions already transcribed using consistent spelling; the
second is a separate list of numbers written on raffle tickets.

\begin{terminal}
practice=$(mktemp -d /tmp/utp20.XXXXXX)
cd "$practice"
printf '%s\n' Chess Knitting Chess Chess Maps Knitting > votes.txt
printf '%s\n' 10 2 1 20 02 > tickets.txt
\end{terminal}

Try \cmd|uniq| alone before using it in a pipeline. With \cmd|-c|
it writes a count at the start of each output line. Leading spaces
are just padding; align your attention on the number and the
suggestion beside it.

\begin{terminal}
uniq -c votes.txt
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
      1 Chess
      1 Knitting
      2 Chess
      1 Maps
      1 Knitting
\end{terminaloutput}

Six votes went in, but Chess still appears in two places. \cmd|uniq|
looks for runs of equal adjacent lines: sections of the input
in which the same line appears without a break. It does not
maintain a running list of everything it has seen. That design is
useful if you need to preserve the order in which things
happened. Consider a list of room changes: Room A, Room A, Room B,
Room A. Collapsing adjacent entries leaves A, B, A, correctly
preserving a return to Room A. Sorting that input first would
erase the information that someone went back.

For the vote count, sequence isn't important, so sort the lines
first. Run \cmd|sort| on its own and check the intermediate
output. It writes to standard output rather than changing the file.

\begin{terminal}
LC_ALL=C sort votes.txt
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
Chess
Chess
Chess
Knitting
Knitting
Maps
\end{terminaloutput}

Now pipe the same result to \cmd|uniq -c|, which reads standard
input since it wasn't given a filename. The order of operations
is the point: the first command makes complete groups, the
second counts those groups.

\begin{terminal}
LC_ALL=C sort votes.txt | LC_ALL=C uniq -c
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
      3 Chess
      2 Knitting
      1 Maps
\end{terminaloutput}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-20/groups.tikz}
\caption{Sorting makes separated duplicates adjacent before counting.}
\label{fig:20-groups}
\VisualDescription{An abbreviated stream shows Chess, Maps, Chess becoming Chess, Chess, Maps after sorting. Counting reduces those two distinct runs to 2 Chess and 1 Maps.}
\end{figure}

Before relying on this count, ask what a line represents. If a
single vote can include two suggestions on one line, this pipeline
counts that pair as a separate choice. If the list contains a
header, it counts the header as a vote. If an entry has two
spaces between words instead of one, character-for-character
comparison in the C locale treats it as a separate line. A tool
can make an exact count of an untidy input. That is very
different from making the count you meant.

\section{Alphabetical order is a policy}
The prefix \cmd|LC_ALL=C| sets the locale for the command that
follows it. A \FirstUse{locale} is a collection of conventions
for interpreting and presenting information. For sorting, one
convention is which characters are compared first and which
are treated similarly. Another is how numbers are written. A
locale may define punctuation or a decimal separator differently
from your usual settings. \cmd|LC_ALL| sets all of the locale
categories for this one command; \texttt{C} is the standard
locale name for predictable traditional behavior. It is available
without installing a language pack. A prefix on the first command
in a pipeline does not automatically apply to each later command.

Use the C locale for exact reproducibility with these plain ASCII
fixtures. It is not a universal recommendation for alphabetizing
names. A person writing a multilingual directory may expect
accented letters close to their unaccented neighbors and not
at the end of the list. In that case, select an installed
locale that meets the reader's needs, record it, and run sample
comparisons including the actual characters that matter. If
you choose a named locale that isn't installed, do not
assume the process used it. The shell does not verify whether
a command can honor the value supplied to it.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' ant Zoo Apple zoo | LC_ALL=C sort
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
Apple
Zoo
ant
zoo
\end{terminaloutput}

In the C locale, uppercase ASCII letters sort before lowercase
ones, so \texttt{Zoo} precedes \texttt{ant}. The terminal didn't
misread the words. You have asked for a different ordering
policy from the one you might use in a subject index. Use
\cmd|locale| to inspect the current setting, or \cmd|locale -a|
to list the available names. A locale does not repair damaged
encoding or automatically convert one kind of stored Unicode
text to another. Do not assume visual equality implies identical
bytes (Chapter~\ref{ch:18}).

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.25in}Y}
\toprule
What matters & First check \\
\midrule
Same report on two machines & Set the comparison locale explicitly and use the same fixture bytes. \\
Good order for readers & Test the intended locale with case, accents and punctuation that occur in the list. \\
Exact duplicates only & Prefer whole-line comparison in the C locale; don't ignore case or compare on a partial key. \\
Grouping by a shared meaning & Agree on a spelling policy, inspect variants and normalize a copy before counting. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Digits on paper, numbers in a comparison}
The tickets list contains \texttt{1}, \texttt{2}, \texttt{10},
\texttt{20} and \texttt{02}. If you're ordering ticket quantities
as numbers, ten should follow two. If you're ordering labels,
you may want \texttt{02} to be distinct from \texttt{2}.
See both interpretations, then decide what you're sorting.

\begin{terminal}
LC_ALL=C sort tickets.txt
LC_ALL=C sort -n tickets.txt
\end{terminal}

The plain sort orders the strings \texttt{02}, \texttt{1},
\texttt{10}, \texttt{2}, \texttt{20}. It compares from the
left; in the comparison between \texttt{10} and \texttt{2},
the first differing digits are 1 and 2. With \cmd|-n|, sort
compares the leading quantities numerically: first 1, then
both spellings of 2, then 10 and 20. The numerical comparison
doesn't turn \texttt{02} into \texttt{2} on output. These are
still the original lines, which can preserve meaningful
differences in a catalog or ticket roll.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-20/numbers.tikz}
\caption{Text order compares digits as characters; numeric order compares values.}
\label{fig:20-numbers}
\VisualDescription{The strings 1, 10 and 2 appear in that order under text comparison, but numeric comparison swaps 10 and 2.}
\end{figure}

Use \cmd|-r| to reverse the comparison, so a numeric maximum
can come first. Here the next sort receives the output of the
tally; the count at the start of each line is the numeric
value it compares. Try deleting the last stage to see that
the tally still worked but wasn't ranked. In this original
fixture the counts happened to appear in descending order
already; the added votes make the change visible.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' Maps Maps Maps >> votes.txt
LC_ALL=C sort votes.txt | LC_ALL=C uniq -c | LC_ALL=C sort -nr
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
      4 Maps
      3 Chess
      2 Knitting
\end{terminaloutput}

The short options \cmd|-n| and \cmd|-r| can be written
together as \cmd|-nr| here. The redirection \cmd|>>|
appends rather than truncating; do not run the append twice
unless you mean to add six votes. If you did repeat it, recreate
only the practice fixture from the setup, after checking
your working directory. On real data, keep the original and
make a separate report. You might need to change the counting
rules or reconstruct the tally later.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.2in}Y}
\toprule
Operation & What it changes \\
\midrule
\texttt{sort} & Reorders lines; by itself it doesn't remove them. \\
\texttt{uniq -c} & Emits one line per adjacent run and prefixes its size. \\
\texttt{sort -nr} & Ranks leading numbers from largest to smallest. \\
\texttt{sort -k 2,2} & Compares by the second blank-delimited field as the selected key. \\
\texttt{sort -u} & Emits one line per equal comparison key; it is not just a different spelling of counting. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Don't confuse a key with a record}
A \FirstUse{sort key} is the portion you ask sort to compare
first. For a list such as \texttt{10 Chess}, a key can pick
out the activity and leave the number alone. \cmd|-k 2,2|
starts at field 2 and stops at the end of field 2. In this
simple list, fields are separated by blanks, and each activity
name is one word. Leaving off the second \texttt{2} would let
the key extend to the end of the line.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' '10 Maps' '2 Chess' '7 Knitting' |
  LC_ALL=C sort -k 2,2
\end{terminal}

The activities should be listed as Chess, Knitting, Maps, even
though the count appears first in each line. Choosing a key
doesn't project or delete fields. \cmd|sort| can still use a
whole-line comparison to order ties unless another option
changes that behavior. To give a report a known secondary
ordering, specify a second key: for example,
\cmd|sort -k 1,1nr -k 2,2| compares the first field numerically
in reverse order, then compares the second field normally.
Here the letters after \texttt{1,1} modify that key alone.
For names of several words, this choice covers only the first
word of the name; decide whether the key should instead extend
through the remaining text.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' '02' '2' | LC_ALL=C sort -n | LC_ALL=C uniq -c
printf '%s\n' '02' '2' | LC_ALL=C sort -n -u
\end{terminal}

The first pipeline counts two distinct original lines once
each. The second prints one representative because those
two numeric keys compare equal. Don't rely on which spelling
is kept for tied keys by a recipe that doesn't promise a
representative. If you need to audit which spelling was
originally used, don't drop either line.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-20/keys.tikz}
\caption{The key may be equal even though the records differ.}
\label{fig:20-keys}
\VisualDescription{Two distinct strings, 02 and 2, point to the same numeric value 2. Their original spellings are still different.}
\end{figure}

\begin{dialoguebox}{One tally or two?}
\Brian{My list contains 02 and 2. Are they duplicates?}
\Stephen{If they are quantities, the numeric comparison makes them equal.}
\Brian{But they're ticket identifiers, and the printer issued both.}
\Stephen{Then I chose the wrong comparison. Keep their spelling intact.}
\Brian{So the same cleaning pipeline could erase a valid ticket?}
\Stephen{Yes. Numerical order is harmless by itself, but keeping just one representative can lose information.}
\Brian{Then I'll decide what a record means before I remove any.}
\DialogueConclusion{Define equality before you remove duplicates.}
\end{dialoguebox}

Be careful with loose input such as \texttt{about 20}, a blank
line, or a date split by hyphens. A numeric sort is not a numeric
validator. It can produce a plausible report even when its
numeric comparison used only part of a line or no digits.
Likewise, a tidy count does not tell you whether every source
was read. Check the upstream steps separately while developing
the pipeline, and check the input against what you know about
it. For the expanded tally, all counts add to nine. They should
agree with \cmd|wc -l votes.txt| because each fixture vote ends
with a newline and none has been omitted. That check won't find a
wrong label, but it will catch some dropped or extra lines.

\begin{furtherreading}
\ReadingItem{POSIX sort}{Keys, numeric and reverse comparison, uniqueness and locale variables}{https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/utilities/sort.html}
\ReadingItem{POSIX uniq}{Adjacent runs and counts}{https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/utilities/uniq.html}
\end{furtherreading}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Group before counting if original sequence no longer matters.
\item Treat locale, comparison keys and ties as explicit report decisions.
\item Keep the raw file; a successful tally is not a check of its meaning.
\end{takeaways}

\begin{exercise}{Insist on a second key}
\label{ex:20-tie}
Rank the quantities \texttt{3 Maps}, \texttt{3 Chess}, and
\texttt{1 Knitting} by quantity descending and activity
ascending. The tie should resolve to Chess first. Nothing is
saved; there is no separate cleanup.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' '3 Maps' '3 Chess' '1 Knitting' |
  LC_ALL=C sort -k 1,1nr -k 2,2
\end{terminal}
Expect Chess, Maps, Knitting in that order, with counts preserved.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Count without reordering}
\label{ex:20-runs}
Represent four consecutive room measurements as A, A, B, A.
Collapse each adjacent run to one line without losing the
return to A. This read-only pipeline creates no files.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' A A B A | LC_ALL=C uniq -c
\end{terminal}
Expect 2 A, 1 B, 1 A. Putting sort first would have hidden the return.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Keep spellings distinct}
\label{ex:20-spelling}
Count distinct full-line ticket labels in \texttt{tickets.txt}.
Expect five different labels, each with a count of 1.
No output file is needed.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
LC_ALL=C sort tickets.txt | LC_ALL=C uniq -c
\end{terminal}
Confirm that both \texttt{02} and \texttt{2} appear.
\end{solution}

